WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.000
Earlier this year, the bracket system was rolled&nbsp;
out in the commander format, providing guidelines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.480
for separating decks into different rough&nbsp;
groupings to be played together. Soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.360
though, I noticed a problem. I noticed a problem—I&nbsp;
didn’t have any bracket two decks. I had a lovely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.840
variety of different bracket 3 decks from&nbsp;
battlecruiser to aggro to control to low-slung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.960
staxy midrange, but nothing I’d feel comfortable&nbsp;
consistently pulling out in games against three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.080
precons or equivalent decks, at least not without&nbsp;
the expectation of playing a 1v3 archenemy sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.840
of role. So, that seemed like a problem worth&nbsp;
remedying. Now, I had built a low-power deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.880
in the past—a couple years previously I’d built a&nbsp;
Skeleton Ship proliferate deck, and that was kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.680
of funny, but it was lower power purely because&nbsp;
it ran a hot garbage commander and a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.600
hot garbage cards in the 99 such as Shrine of&nbsp;
Piercing Vision, Mindless Automaton, Time Bomb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.360
and a whole four of the 90s storage lands that&nbsp;
couldn’t tap for non-stored mana. It was funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.080
but the play pattern was jarring, with the deck&nbsp;
being glacially slow to get going but potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.400
quite oppressive in the late game if it was&nbsp;
left alone to fully build up an engine. You can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.200
probably imagine why I mostly stopped playing this&nbsp;
deck. One alternative option for having a bracket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:14.640
2 deck would be just buying a precon, and maybe&nbsp;
doing a slight round of upgrades or sidegrades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.520
but I wanted something that felt more my own&nbsp;
than that. There’s also another problem with both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:26.000
precons and the Skeleton Ship deck—they feel kinda&nbsp;
suboptimal. This probably sounds like a very silly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.280
criticism of a deck intended to play in relatively&nbsp;
low-power games, but I really like the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.080
of tightening up a deck’s cohesion, of finding&nbsp;
the perfect cards for its mechanical needs, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:42.080
process of turning the deck into a machine that&nbsp;
does something. I like that process and I like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.800
play decks that feel like they’re performing the&nbsp;
role I want them to perform as well as they can.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.200
Immediately this is a bit of a conflict—this&nbsp;
preference for optimization works fine for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.920
bracket 3, because there are a whole lot of&nbsp;
somewhat funky ideas whose optimized version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.200
ends up in that sort of nice mid-power zone.&nbsp;
But bracket 2 is harder—what sort of idea is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:07.040
so inherently mediocre that optimizing it ends up&nbsp;
at the power level of a precon? In this case, I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.480
actually took inspiration from precons themselves.&nbsp;
See, preconstructed commander decks are typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.680
unfocused by design—they feature slightly too&nbsp;
many themes and elements because they’re trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:22.640
give players a taste of different playstyles, and&nbsp;
also give some easy paths to upgrade a given deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.480
such as cutting a singular subtheme for some more&nbsp;
relevant stuff. So, what if I did the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:34.240
but on overdrive: what if I built a deck&nbsp;
with way too many subthemes, like a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.440
irresponsible number of subthemes, and then&nbsp;
tried my hardest to optimize it into a workable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:45.520
decklist? Thus began the process of building&nbsp;
Phyrexian-flavored Subtheme Soup. Longtime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:50.160
Snail heads out there might remember that I had a&nbsp;
Glissa Sunslayer battlecruiser deck a while back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.520
and while I love Glissa as a character, both&nbsp;
that deck and that Glissa ran a bit generic for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.320
my tastes. Glissa the Traitor could be fun&nbsp;
as an old-school sort of commander choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.280
but that one is actually a bit too specific for&nbsp;
my purposes her, with a setup and payoff that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:10.320
don’t offer a super wide variety of options.&nbsp;
However, there’s another green black option,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:16.160
and that one actually lands right in the sweet&nbsp;
spot for me—Glissa, Herald of Predation. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.080
a 5 mana 3/5 that offers a choice at the beginning&nbsp;
of combat on your turn—Incubate 2 twice, flip all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:27.920
your incubator tokens, or give your Phyrexians&nbsp;
first strike and deathtouch. Incubate as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.200
mechanic is pretty unique—it creates a noncreature&nbsp;
artifact token with +1/+1 counters on it that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.400
can later be flipped into a Phyrexian artifact&nbsp;
creature by paying two mana. Obviously this lends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.440
itself to a deck that cares about counters,&nbsp;
so we’ll call that subtheme #1, but honestly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.800
the first thing I noticed about Glissa is “wow&nbsp;
this card generates a lot of artifact tokens”.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:53.120
There’s a number of fairly straightforward ways&nbsp;
to take advantage of having artifact tokens. Cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.600
like Tarrian’s Journal and Eviscerator’s Insight&nbsp;
sacrifice artifacts to draw, Monumental Corruption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.920
can either be a big draw spell or a player-killing&nbsp;
spell depending on the artifact count, and Throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.800
of Geth is a potent one-card engine with the&nbsp;
commander, also boosting the counters theme. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:11.920
for our purposes here, having a lot of artifacts&nbsp;
is subtheme #2, and it’s one of the more central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:17.360
themes here. It also contains arguably the deck’s&nbsp;
most powerful card, and one that inspired much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:22.960
of the rest of the list—Clock of Omens. This is a&nbsp;
four mana artifact that allows a player to tap two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.960
artifacts to untap a different artifact, and while&nbsp;
it’s not quite a combo piece here—I think—it still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.760
has the potential for some big turns. The most&nbsp;
basic version of that is with some of my bigger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.880
counter-based mana rocks—repeatedly tapping and&nbsp;
untapping Everflowing Chalice, Astral Cornucopia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.760
or Empowered Autogenerator can really push the&nbsp;
deck’s mana into new territory and let it do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.280
some big plays. There are a lot of other options&nbsp;
as well, though—Idol of Oblivion for card draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.920
Staff of Compleation for proliferates and&nbsp;
more, and both Twitching Doll and Lux Cannon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.960
as cool cards that also use counters. The deck&nbsp;
contains a couple other options for untapping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.280
but it does sometimes run into a problem—not&nbsp;
all of the cool tap abilities I want to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:08.320
are on artifacts. I have cards like Plaguemaw&nbsp;
Beast and Wight of the Reliquary that take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.320
advantage of my tokens, and I’d quite like&nbsp;
to activate them repeatedly after flipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:17.520
a bunch of incubators with Glissa, so…&nbsp;
what if I just turned them into artifacts?

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.640
Enter the liquimetal package. This features&nbsp;
Liquimetal Torque and Phyrexian Scriptures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.520
both cards that can turn a creature into an&nbsp;
artifact creature, as well as being a mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:31.760
rock and a board wipe, respectively. This&nbsp;
package also contains two other cards, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:38.480
these are actually my two favorite cards in the&nbsp;
deck—Dollhouse of Horrors and Nexus of Becoming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.040
Both of these create token copies of cards&nbsp;
that are also artifact creature constructs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.000
with Dollhouse doing this by exiling creatures&nbsp;
from the graveyard and Nexus doing this by exiling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:53.520
artifacts or creatures from a player’s hand. The&nbsp;
downside of exiling the copied card is certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:59.440
real, but the potential volume of liquimetal-ed&nbsp;
creature creation is really potent here. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:04.400
due to a particular quirk of these cards, they’re&nbsp;
able to form the bedrock for yet another one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:10.000
the deck’s subthemes—token copy populate. Since&nbsp;
the artifact creature copies created by Nexus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:15.520
and Dollhouse are tokens, they can be further&nbsp;
copied by cards like Selesnya Eulogist and Esika’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.400
Chariot, cards that copy tokens. This can allow&nbsp;
for some nutty levels of advantage building, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.200
to improve the odds for things to come together&nbsp;
I threw in a few extra token copy generators as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:30.160
well. This still isn’t a huge package—just five&nbsp;
setup and four payoff—but most of these cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.960
are fine enough on their own, and the payoff&nbsp;
for putting them together can be pretty huge.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.480
A late edition to this deck was a graveyard&nbsp;
subtheme. I adore Dollhouse of Horrors and on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.280
paper it’s perfect for this deck, but too often&nbsp;
in my early games I was finding myself without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.000
much to do with it, making copies of maybe one or&nbsp;
two creatures but not feeling like I was getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:55.200
the full potential out of the card. This was&nbsp;
also my experience with another card I like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.680
Vat of Rebirth, so I decided to help these cards&nbsp;
out with the addition of some other graveyard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.480
payoffs and especially some more graveyard&nbsp;
enablers. I went with mostly pretty flexible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:08.960
options here—Millikin as a card that’s also&nbsp;
a mana dork, Sarinth Steelseeker as a sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.360
of value engine that works well with my artifact&nbsp;
tokens, Old Rutstein as a card that also itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:19.520
pushes my tokens plan, and Tyvar as a planeswalker&nbsp;
that also ties into activated abilities. With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:23.680
graveyard package covered we’ve capped off the&nbsp;
list of all the major subthemes, but there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.120
a few more minor packages as well. The deck runs&nbsp;
a constructs package with Scrapyard Recombiner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:34.400
which gives it access to a removal spell and a&nbsp;
few synergy options of different calibers. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:39.040
also five cards that create powerstones, which&nbsp;
can pay for one of the deck’s 31 artifacts, one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.400
the deck’s 23 cards with a colorless-mana-payable&nbsp;
activated ability, or any of the incubator tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.400
Glissa makes. For the weirdest little tidbit here,&nbsp;
the deck can actually populate incubator tokens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.760
This might seem bad since the copy won’t enter&nbsp;
with counters and will instantly die when flipped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:00.400
or instantly instantly if it’s already on the&nbsp;
creature side, but this actually allows for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.440
on-demand usage of on-death trigger cards&nbsp;
like Dross Scorpion and Morbid Opportunist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.800
without needing a sac outlet. Sort of like&nbsp;
aristocrats without the aristocrats, I guess.

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.880
So this deck kind of does a little bit of&nbsp;
everything, and all of that everything is anchored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:19.760
by Glissa. The artifact tokens she provides are&nbsp;
certainly a big help for sacrificing and whatnot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:24.880
but the biggest thing Glissa does is provide the&nbsp;
deck with a reliable wincon. Typically I’ll try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:30.080
to ramp out Glissa on turn 4 and start gradually&nbsp;
accumulating incubators, maybe sacrifice one here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.480
or there. Once that’s up and chugging, I can just&nbsp;
focus on cobbling together whatever stupid little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:38.880
engine I have the tools to create, whether that be&nbsp;
a value engine, a ramp engine, a removal engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.880
or some combination of these things. What’s&nbsp;
important is that I eventually find my way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.520
to some proliferate, at which point I can use my&nbsp;
resource advantage to amplify the counters on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:53.120
accumulated tokens before having Glissa flip them,&nbsp;
allowing me to swing big at an opponent. The fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:57.760
that such an absurdly complex and multifaceted&nbsp;
deck typically wins by swinging in with a bunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:03.840
of vanilla 6/6s is a pretty juicy morsel of irony,&nbsp;
and it really lets the deck feel truly belonging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.960
in bracket 2. And actually, this feature about&nbsp;
the deck is part of what helps it stay comfortably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:14.000
bracket 2. In theory this deck has some gross&nbsp;
plays—first of all, there’s an infinite mana combo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.240
that involves popping out Empowered Autogenerator&nbsp;
as a creature with Nexus of Becoming before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:23.120
repeatedly untapping it with Voltaic Construct&nbsp;
the following turn. It’s a rather strange one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.640
and requires a nontrivial amount of setup, but&nbsp;
it is still a three card combo. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.040
the deck features pretty powerful cards&nbsp;
like Clock of Omens, Kudoltha Forgemaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.760
and Staff of Compleation, and these can help the&nbsp;
deck assemble some pretty disgusting engines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.480
Despite this, I have not once had a win with&nbsp;
this deck that didn’t heavily involve Glissa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:46.320
and that puts a natural cap on things. As&nbsp;
long as she’s out, I get two incubators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.800
per turn every turn, but that’s all I get. I can&nbsp;
make them bigger—in some cases a lot bigger if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.200
I had a proliferate engine really pop off—but&nbsp;
I’m still going to be winning the game with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:59.920
fairly ordinary and extremely predictable&nbsp;
number of most-likely-vanilla creatures.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.600
So that’s the deck, and I do find it&nbsp;
quite fun, but I worry that the process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.800
of describing it might be making it seem a bit&nbsp;
more functional than it actually is. A deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:13.360
tries to cram in as many subthemes as possible&nbsp;
is naturally going to struggle with dead cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.360
when the cards intended to work with those&nbsp;
cards decide not to show up. The only deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:21.600
I have that comes even close to this one&nbsp;
in terms of complexity is my Sharuum deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:26.320
but there’s a pretty clear difference between&nbsp;
these two—that deck is a toolbox deck that runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.760
11 conditional tutors to help it piece together&nbsp;
engines, whereas this deck has two tutors. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:35.920
don’t know if there’s been an exact tutor limit&nbsp;
given for bracket 2, but I’ve heard the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.360
3 floated around and that felt like a nice&nbsp;
number to shoot for here that would force some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.760
creative workarounds without being an outright&nbsp;
barrier against tutoring. All this to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:47.760
with the daring vision and self-imposed&nbsp;
restrictions I had going into this deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:53.040
dead cards were inevitably going to be a&nbsp;
challenge. So, how does this deck mitigate that?

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:58.560
A big one is that I try pretty hard not to let&nbsp;
a card perform only one task for the deck. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:02.800
is no generic removal here—when I highlight the&nbsp;
cards in my interaction suite that proliferate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.720
use counters, or are lands in Archidekt, that&nbsp;
excludes only five cards, and all of those either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:13.600
fit into another subtheme or have some sort of&nbsp;
useful synergy in a removal engine. The same goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:18.800
for the ramp—of the deck’s 19 cards that perform&nbsp;
some sort of ramp function, the two most generic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:23.600
are Golgari Signet and Talisman of Resilience,&nbsp;
with every single other one involving counters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:28.400
producing powerstones, or otherwise slotting&nbsp;
into one of the deck’s themes. Likewise for draw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:32.640
with Morbid Opportunist being by far the most&nbsp;
generic of the deck’s 17 sources of value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.800
and that’s even considering the weird populate&nbsp;
trick I mentioned earlier. Something else you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:40.800
might notice here is that the number of&nbsp;
ramp and draw spells are fairly high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:45.200
and this is another pretty essential feature&nbsp;
for letting the deck run smoothly. Since there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:49.840
are single digit counts of setup and payoff for a&nbsp;
number of different subthemes throughout the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:54.640
it becomes very important to see more of the&nbsp;
deck in order to piece together engines. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.240
actually bought a couple of draw engines on the&nbsp;
convention floor at MagicCon Vegas because I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:02.400
noticing some light stallouts in the games&nbsp;
I was playing, and sure enough that helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:06.480
the deck out quite a bit. This also applies&nbsp;
to ramp—since there’s no guarantee that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.800
low-cost cards I have available will be what I’m&nbsp;
looking for in a given moment, having some extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:15.760
ramp to let me use activated abilities and more&nbsp;
expensive cards instead also contributes a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:20.400
flexibility. Having a high count of ramp is also&nbsp;
quite helpful since the deck has a relatively high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.560
mana commander that’s relatively central to its&nbsp;
gameplan, and always having the mana to replay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:29.760
her kinda lets me avoid needing to think too hard&nbsp;
about how consistent my alternate wincons are.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:34.560
Despite this effort, there are still ongoing&nbsp;
challenges here. Some subthemes just don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:38.240
really have cards that are good on their&nbsp;
own, or which tie into other themes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:43.520
Selesnya Eulogist and Rootcast Apprenticeship are&nbsp;
somewhat subpar interaction if I don’t have token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:48.800
copies, the two non-clock untapping effects&nbsp;
are both fairly restrictive in their effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:53.680
even if their ceiling is high. And, that’s to&nbsp;
say nothing of the graveyard package. I might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:57.360
have made it sound like I had the problem of&nbsp;
not enough cards for Dollhouse to reanimate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:02.320
and I solved it by adding 4 or 5 graveyard&nbsp;
enablers, but honestly it still feels like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:07.600
barely enough—the card still runs out of useful&nbsp;
stuff to copy pretty regularly. In addition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:12.560
there’s another similar card I want to add to this&nbsp;
deck—God-Pharaoh’s Gift has a higher upfront cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:16.800
but is otherwise pretty similar to Dollhouse&nbsp;
of Horrors, making token copies of creatures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:22.160
in graveyard by exiling them. This is another one&nbsp;
of my favorite cards, but it currently makes no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:26.880
sense at all here—it’d have the same problems as&nbsp;
Dollhouse in not having enough targets to copy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:31.040
and it doesn’t even tie into the tap/untap,&nbsp;
liquimetal, or construct subthemes like Dollhouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:36.080
does. There are two problems with the graveyard&nbsp;
package currently—only 6 cards that put cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:41.360
into the yard at the moment, and only 23 total&nbsp;
creatures in the deck. It’s kinda discouraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:47.040
to do the math on this—if I’ve gone through 20&nbsp;
cards of my deck, I’ve only seen 4 or 5 creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:50.880
and the number of those that have made their way&nbsp;
to the graveyard is almost certainly less than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:55.360
that. Current goals for the deck are increasing&nbsp;
the creature count, finding a third tutor for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.360 --> 00:14:00.960
deck that helps alleviate current challenges, and&nbsp;
possibly fleshing out other packages as well. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:05.680
maybeboard on archidekt goes kinda crazy—I&nbsp;
have four different categories of possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:10.640
additions within the maybeboard, in addition to&nbsp;
a more generic category. If you have ideas for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:15.680
how to cram more deck into this deck, please&nbsp;
let me know. I’d genuinely love to hear them.

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:19.920
It’s funny when I think about what advice I’d&nbsp;
give if somebody else presented this deck to me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:24.720
saying they wanted to make it better. Because, the&nbsp;
answer is obviously “please cut some subthemes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:29.040
you have way too many of them.” Why am I spending&nbsp;
deck space on tap/untap cards other than Clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:33.840
of Omens? Why am I going to the effort of a&nbsp;
copy populate package? Why am I bothering to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:39.440
integrate graveyard synergies into this? Why am&nbsp;
I even thinking about aristocratless aristocrats?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.680
A sensible player trying to improve this would&nbsp;
cut most of the graveyard support, at least two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:49.120
of the populate cards, and all of the token copy&nbsp;
cards aside from Nexus of Becoming. But that’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:53.840
joy of this deck to me—I shouldn’t be doing what&nbsp;
I’m doing if I wanted a deck that plays smoothly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:58.320
and the fact that I’m doing it anyway is what&nbsp;
makes this a bracket 2 deck. It’s the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:02.960
reason this deck includes some kinda-not-great&nbsp;
proliferate cards. Would a sensible player trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:07.680
to upgrade this deck include Plaguemaw Beast,&nbsp;
Carnivorous Canopy, and Spread the Sickness?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:12.000
Probably not! But it’s not like I’m going to&nbsp;
be playing these flavorful cards anywhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:17.920
and here they’re… fine. They feel acceptable.&nbsp;
And that’s pretty cool if you ask me.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:21.280
Thanks for watching! I talked a bit about&nbsp;
my Skeleton Ship deck in this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:25.600
and if you want to know more about that I posted&nbsp;
a short video for supporters discussing that deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:29.280
its play patterns, its pitfalls, and&nbsp;
some of the dumbest games I had with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:33.120
If you want the full version, you can join my&nbsp;
Patreon or become a YouTube member, but there will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:38.320
be a sneak preview for it in about 10 seconds.&nbsp;
I also released an older patron video about my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:42.560
original Glissa deck over on my 2nd channel,&nbsp;
with some included commentary of why I swapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:49.840
it out for a new deck, so you can also check that&nbsp;
out. With that, it’s time to enter the bone zone.

